    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "ring from the given node: 3 -> 4 -> 1,  insertVal = 2   ->  3 -> 4 -> 1 -> 2\nring: 3 -> 5 -> 1,  insertVal = 6                       ->  3 -> 5 -> 6 -> 1\nring: 3 -> 3 -> 3,  insertVal = 4                       ->  3 -> 3 -> 3 -> 4  (anywhere)\nempty,  insertVal = 1                                   ->  1 (pointing to itself)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: three places a value can go"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Walk round the ring with two pointers, `prev` and `curr = prev.next`, looking at each **gap** between neighbours. A sorted ring has exactly one place where the values **drop** — from the largest back to the smallest. That gives three cases:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**1. Between two values:** `prev.val ≤ x ≤ curr.val` — insert here. **2. At the drop** (`prev.val > curr.val`): if `x` is a new largest (`x ≥ prev.val`) or a new smallest (`x ≤ curr.val`), it goes between the largest and the smallest — insert here. **3. All the way round** without either case: every value is the same, so any gap works. In every case, insertion is the same two pointer changes: `prev.next = node; node.next = curr`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Find the smallest node first, then walk up from it"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function insert(head, insertVal) {\n  const node = new ListNode(insertVal);\n  if (head === null) {\n    node.next = node;\n    return node;\n  }\n  let max = head;\n  while (max.next !== head && max.val <= max.next.val) max = max.next;   // pass 1: the largest node\n  const min = max.next;\n  let prev = max;                                   // a new max or min goes between max and min\n  if (insertVal > min.val && insertVal < max.val) {\n    prev = min;                                     // pass 2: walk up from the smallest\n    while (prev.next.val < insertVal) prev = prev.next;\n  }\n  node.next = prev.next;\n  prev.next = node;\n  return head;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def insert(head, insert_val):\n    node = ListNode(insert_val)\n    if head is None:\n        node.next = node\n        return node\n    mx = head\n    while mx.next is not head and mx.val <= mx.next.val:   # pass 1: the largest node\n        mx = mx.next\n    mn = mx.next\n    prev = mx                                    # a new max or min goes between max and min\n    if mn.val < insert_val < mx.val:\n        prev = mn                                # pass 2: walk up from the smallest\n        while prev.next.val < insert_val:\n            prev = prev.next\n    node.next = prev.next\n    prev.next = node\n    return head"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "ListNode insert(ListNode head, int insertVal) {\n    ListNode node = new ListNode(insertVal);\n    if (head == null) {\n        node.next = node;\n        return node;\n    }\n    ListNode max = head;\n    while (max.next != head && max.val <= max.next.val) max = max.next;   // pass 1: the largest node\n    ListNode min = max.next;\n    ListNode prev = max;                              // a new max or min goes between max and min\n    if (insertVal > min.val && insertVal < max.val) {\n        prev = min;                                   // pass 2: walk up from the smallest\n        while (prev.next.val < insertVal) prev = prev.next;\n    }\n    node.next = prev.next;\n    prev.next = node;\n    return head;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "ListNode* insert(ListNode* head, int insertVal) {\n    ListNode* node = new ListNode(insertVal);\n    if (head == nullptr) {\n        node->next = node;\n        return node;\n    }\n    ListNode* mx = head;\n    while (mx->next != head && mx->val <= mx->next->val) mx = mx->next;   // pass 1: the largest node\n    ListNode* mn = mx->next;\n    ListNode* prev = mx;                              // a new max or min goes between max and min\n    if (insertVal > mn->val && insertVal < mx->val) {\n        prev = mn;                                    // pass 2: walk up from the smallest\n        while (prev->next->val < insertVal) prev = prev->next;\n    }\n    node->next = prev->next;\n    prev->next = node;\n    return head;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1)** — but up to two passes. Turning the ring back into a normal sorted list (find where it starts) is the instinctive move; the next version notices you don't need to."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. One pass, three cases"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "insert-sorted-circular",
      "caption": "Check each gap: between two values, at the drop, or all the way round."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function insert(head, insertVal) {\n  const node = new ListNode(insertVal);\n  if (head === null) {                 // empty: a ring of one\n    node.next = node;\n    return node;\n  }\n  let prev = head, curr = head.next;\n  do {\n    if (prev.val <= insertVal && insertVal <= curr.val) break;   // fits between two values\n    if (prev.val > curr.val && (insertVal >= prev.val || insertVal <= curr.val)) break;   // at the drop: a new max or min\n    prev = curr;\n    curr = curr.next;\n  } while (prev !== head);             // all the way round: every value is equal, so anywhere works\n  prev.next = node;                    // insert between prev and curr\n  node.next = curr;\n  return head;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def insert(head, insert_val):\n    node = ListNode(insert_val)\n    if head is None:                     # empty: a ring of one\n        node.next = node\n        return node\n    prev, curr = head, head.next\n    while True:\n        if prev.val <= insert_val <= curr.val:          # fits between two values\n            break\n        if prev.val > curr.val and (insert_val >= prev.val or insert_val <= curr.val):\n            break                                       # at the drop: a new max or min\n        prev, curr = curr, curr.next\n        if prev is head:                 # all the way round: every value is equal\n            break\n    prev.next = node                     # insert between prev and curr\n    node.next = curr\n    return head"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "ListNode insert(ListNode head, int insertVal) {\n    ListNode node = new ListNode(insertVal);\n    if (head == null) {                  // empty: a ring of one\n        node.next = node;\n        return node;\n    }\n    ListNode prev = head, curr = head.next;\n    do {\n        if (prev.val <= insertVal && insertVal <= curr.val) break;   // fits between two values\n        if (prev.val > curr.val && (insertVal >= prev.val || insertVal <= curr.val)) break;   // at the drop: a new max or min\n        prev = curr;\n        curr = curr.next;\n    } while (prev != head);              // all the way round: every value is equal, so anywhere works\n    prev.next = node;                    // insert between prev and curr\n    node.next = curr;\n    return head;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "ListNode* insert(ListNode* head, int insertVal) {\n    ListNode* node = new ListNode(insertVal);\n    if (head == nullptr) {               // empty: a ring of one\n        node->next = node;\n        return node;\n    }\n    ListNode* prev = head;\n    ListNode* curr = head->next;\n    do {\n        if (prev->val <= insertVal && insertVal <= curr->val) break;   // fits between two values\n        if (prev->val > curr->val && (insertVal >= prev->val || insertVal <= curr->val)) break;   // at the drop: a new max or min\n        prev = curr;\n        curr = curr->next;\n    } while (prev != head);              // all the way round: every value is equal, so anywhere works\n    prev->next = node;                   // insert between prev and curr\n    node->next = curr;\n    return head;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1)** in a single pass. The `do … while` checks the gap after the head first and stops after exactly one lap — the circular-list version of **stop when you're back where you started**."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Find the smallest node first, then walk up from it",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "Up to **two passes**: one to find the start, one to find the spot.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just a few pointers."
        },
        {
          "approach": "One pass, three cases",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "**Exactly one lap**, checking each gap once.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just a few pointers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Both are linear; the one-pass version is shorter and is the answer interviewers expect. The three cases — **between**, **at the drop**, **all equal** — are the part worth remembering."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Empty list** — return a new node that points to itself.",
        "**One node** — the new node and the old one point at each other.",
        "**A new largest or smallest value** — it must go at the drop, not after the given node.",
        "**All values equal** — no gap qualifies; the lap must end and insert anyway, instead of looping forever.",
        "**Returning the new node instead of the given one** — the caller expects the node they passed in (unless the list was empty)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Insert into a sorted singly linked list** — the same **between two values** check, plus a special case for the new head.",
        "**Merge two sorted circular lists** — cut each open at its smallest node, merge, and close the result.",
        "**Sorted circular doubly linked list** — the same cases, with four pointer changes for the insert."
      ]
    }
